#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc280e-d5f1-5a84-9cfc-9c3ad50813fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482920} {:answer/id #uuid "7228d70a-0771-5134-8636-ee3ba6b53f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483011} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e6b23f-d37b-563e-840d-2a93839faacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483179} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bafb54-6e90-57a9-943c-bbd0971097f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "112e5b58-a689-5138-b33f-e47c56e7167c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482839} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3adf1-3aa8-506a-a4e2-98cf256eb5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4126f1c5-7567-585d-9e49-d37c72e8e18c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162753" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4x-2y+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 2,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2,1),\\,(2,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(2,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-9$ | $11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2,1),$ and its minimum value is $-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9bf5ef-0b2f-58e3-a02b-38b0b94d9f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278848} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f417c7-1adf-5968-be5e-7a28cd0f3ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280219} {:answer/id #uuid "c67face3-ea0f-54ec-abf8-31d98bb676cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280331} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1ed97-fd98-5ee9-abbe-fa7c46a9977b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} :answer-field/id #uuid "40847485-fda7-51d7-b82e-0e2ddf1864ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "932d87ff-4a97-5b0b-b8d7-da2ba43e7f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162763" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf225b47-4c19-5217-8990-347551b6707b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372204} {:answer/id #uuid "56197474-e8f7-5ce5-8d46-2baf9e825918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372205} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2340ab-e19e-52ae-b570-eac281dc9ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372206} {:answer/id #uuid "3454ace2-65ba-5ed8-8b19-9b8ae2dca207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372207} {:answer/id #uuid "60ca4825-53f4-5526-a63c-844efa53d479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ca4825-53f4-5526-a63c-844efa53d479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372208} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a272c34-6771-5a9a-abbf-859c9c9ddcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b81c4517-112b-539a-a190-05f9b7ce0159" :question/math-academy-id "q-162774" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=12x+4\\sqrt{6}y\\,$ on the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨12,4\\sqrt{6}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}12=6\\lambda x \\\\ 4\\sqrt{6}=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=6$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{12+12}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-24\\,$ | $\\,24\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $24.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077e60b6-8dfd-5425-af73-cfd611ef7918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482822} {:answer/id #uuid "53ae6944-c0ae-5fb2-bd44-030a207fcefc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482958} {:answer/id #uuid "70f63dec-0347-57c6-8669-f185a64af767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483009} {:answer/id #uuid "e12ede27-37da-589d-8cba-76441bf6394f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483192} {:answer/id #uuid "fde02425-ec57-5a63-be2e-568b6cad03db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70f63dec-0347-57c6-8669-f185a64af767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483009} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db7cbdb-ccee-5e24-89d3-8d2ca2dc081d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27a9cc77-ffaa-5079-b6d3-f595b1244e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162776" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10x-2\\sqrt{15}y\\,$ on the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-2\\sqrt{15}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨10x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10=10\\lambda x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{15}=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5+15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-20\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-20.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c313004-6208-55c4-a29e-409a760d3bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482914} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd23171-d788-5e6e-8ad6-c9430da94769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482952} {:answer/id #uuid "711f158a-fa20-5db5-8546-777a45f7ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483010} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5be8a-a984-5074-bd2c-9994fe1ca5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483057} {:answer/id #uuid "d28aa3fe-8784-549c-be59-9299e7bbdf0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "711f158a-fa20-5db5-8546-777a45f7ce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483010} :answer-field/id #uuid "110d77ff-11e1-54ff-9518-294a8feb70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0386d5f-8012-5e50-aca9-5585df04ede1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162779" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the parabola ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,-2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-2y-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},-1),(\\sqrt{2},-1).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-2),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-2)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},-1)$ and its minimum value is $3.$"}]}
